window.__hvBdRegister("vitruvius-architecture", {"key":"vitruvius-architecture","sections":[{"i":0,"j":24,"loc":"Book I, Preface–Chapter I","title":"Dedication to Caesar and the architect's education","summary":"Vitruvius dedicates the work to Imperator Caesar, recalling his service to Caesar's father in repairing artillery and the pension renewed on the recommendation of Caesar's sister. He then argues that the architect needs both practice and theory, and must be versed in drawing, geometry, history, philosophy, music, medicine, law and astronomy, giving reasons for each: the Caryatides and Persian Porch for history, tuning catapults and theatre sounding vessels for music. Against Pytheos, who wanted the architect to surpass specialists in every art, he holds that the architect needs only a fair knowledge of the theory common to the arts, not the specialist's practical mastery. He ends by asking pardon for any faults of style, since he writes as an architect, not as a philosopher or rhetorician.","ideas":["Architecture rests on practice joined to theory; either alone fails","The architect needs a broad liberal education, since all studies share a common bond","Ornaments like the Caryatides carry histories the architect should be able to explain","Theory is shared among the arts; practical execution belongs to specialists"],"terms":[{"t":"Caryatides","d":"Statues of robed women used in place of columns, explained as a memorial of the punishment of Caryae for siding with Persia."},{"t":"ēcheia (sounding vessels)","d":"Bronze vessels placed under theatre seats according to musical intervals to strengthen the actor's voice."}]},{"i":25,"j":50,"loc":"Book I, Chapters II–IV","title":"Principles of architecture and choosing a healthy site","summary":"Vitruvius names the six things architecture depends on: Order, Arrangement, Eurythmy, Symmetry, Propriety and Economy, defining each, with Propriety illustrated by matching Doric, Ionic and Corinthian temples to the character of their gods. He divides architecture into building, the making of timepieces and machinery, and states that all buildings must have durability, convenience and beauty. Turning to towns, he demands a high, temperate site free of marshes, explaining health through the balance of the four elements in bodies. He recommends the ancestral practice of inspecting the livers of grazing animals, cites the spleenless cattle of Gortyna, and contrasts healthy seaside marshes with stagnant ones, ending with the removal of Old Salpia to a better site.","ideas":["Six principles: order, arrangement, eurythmy, symmetry, propriety, economy","Durability, convenience and beauty are required of every building","Health of a site depends on climate and on the balance of the four elements in bodies","Animal livers and pasture reveal whether a site's food and water are wholesome"],"terms":[{"t":"symmetry","d":"Agreement of the parts of a work with each other and with the whole, measured from a chosen standard part, as in the human body."},{"t":"propriety","d":"Correctness of style arising from prescription, usage or nature, such as fitting the order of a temple to its god."},{"t":"stoicheia (elements)","d":"Heat, moisture, the earthy and air, whose mixture makes up every animal body."}]},{"i":51,"j":76,"loc":"Book I, Chapters V–VII","title":"City walls, the winds and street planning","summary":"Vitruvius prescribes deep foundations, projecting round or polygonal towers a bowshot apart, approaches that expose an attacker's unshielded right side, walls tied with charred olive wood, and comb-like earthen ramparts. He then explains winds as air driven by heat meeting moisture, shown by the bronze eolipile, and describes Andronicus of Cyrrhus's octagonal Tower of the Winds in Athens. He gives a gnomon-and-compass method for dividing the circle into eight wind sectors and directs streets along the lines between them so that blasts break on the corners of blocks. Answering those who count more winds, he appeals to Eratosthenes' measure of the earth, then closes the book by assigning sites for the forum and the temples of the various gods.","ideas":["Round towers resist battering rams; salient angles shelter the enemy","Streets should be laid out between the quarters of the winds to shut out their force","Eight principal winds, as on the Tower of the Winds, each with lesser neighbouring breezes","Temples are sited by the god's nature, some within and some outside the walls"],"terms":[{"t":"eolipile","d":"A hollow bronze ball of water that blasts out steam when heated, used to show how winds arise."},{"t":"gnomon (skiathēras)","d":"A shadow-casting pointer whose equal morning and afternoon shadows are used to find north and south."}]},{"i":77,"j":96,"loc":"Book II, Introduction–Chapter II","title":"Dinocrates and the origin of building","summary":"Book II opens with Dinocrates, who won Alexander's notice by appearing dressed as Hercules and proposed carving Mount Athos into a man holding a city; Alexander praised the design but rejected the site for lack of cornfields, and later had Dinocrates build Alexandria. Vitruvius, lacking such looks, hopes to win favour by his writings. He then tells how fire first drew primitive men together, giving rise to speech and assembly, and how they built shelters of boughs, mud and twigs, improving them by imitation and rivalry. Surviving rustic houses among the Colchians, Phrygians and others confirm this account. He defends the placing of this book and briefly reviews the physicists' views on the primordial substance before turning to materials.","ideas":["A city needs fields to feed it, as Alexander told Dinocrates","The discovery of fire led to assembly, speech and the first shelters","Primitive building techniques survive among foreign peoples and in old Roman relics","All materials are composed of elementary particles, as the physicists teach"],"terms":[{"t":"Dinocrates","d":"Macedonian architect who proposed shaping Mount Athos into a statue and later built Alexandria."},{"t":"primordial substance","d":"The basic stuff of things: water for Thales, fire for Heraclitus, atoms for Democritus and Epicurus, four elements for the Pythagoreans."}]},{"i":97,"j":123,"loc":"Book II, Chapters III–VII","title":"Brick, sand, lime, pozzolana and stone","summary":"Vitruvius says bricks should be made of smooth chalky or red clay in spring or autumn and dried two years, and describes Lydian, pentadoron and tetradoron bricks and the floating bricks of Spain and Pitane. He ranks pitsand above river and sea sand for masonry, gives mortar proportions, and explains that burning limestone drives out its moisture and air, leaving open pores that bind with sand. The volcanic powder near Baiae and Vesuvius sets hard even under water because fire has dried the soil; Tuscany yields carbuncular sand instead. He classifies quarry stone as soft, medium or hard, praises the fire- and frost-proof Anician stone, and advises quarrying soft stone two years ahead to test it by exposure.","ideas":["Bricks must dry slowly and thoroughly or they crack and pull away from stucco","Fresh pitsand makes the strongest masonry; river sand suits stucco","Fire-dried volcanic soil explains why pozzolana sets under water","Exposure to weather tests which stones are fit for walls above ground"],"terms":[{"t":"pozzolana","d":"A volcanic powder from around Baiae and Vesuvius that, mixed with lime and rubble, hardens even in the sea."},{"t":"pentadoron / tetradoron","d":"Greek bricks five or four palms square, used respectively for public and private buildings."}]},{"i":124,"j":144,"loc":"Book II, Chapter VIII","title":"Methods of walling and the palace of Mausolus","summary":"Vitruvius compares opus reticulatum, handsome but prone to cracking, with the stronger opus incertum, and warns that porous rubble sucks the strength from mortar, causing faced monuments to collapse. He praises Greek walls of hard stone laid in bonded courses, isodomum and pseudisodomum, with through-stones, over Roman facings filled with loose rubble. Brick walls hold their value, and kings like Mausolus built in brick; this leads to a digression on Halicarnassus, the spring of Salmacis, and Artemisia's capture of the Rhodian fleet and of Rhodes. He then explains why Roman law and crowding force tall buildings of stone and burnt brick within the city, advises coping crude brick walls, and condemns wattle and daub as a fire hazard.","ideas":["Courses bonded through the wall's thickness last; faced rubble fill does not","Rubble walls are valued as lasting eighty years, brick walls at full cost","Artemisia used Mausolus's hidden harbour to trap the Rhodians and take Rhodes","Limited space makes Rome build high, which crude brick walls cannot support"],"terms":[{"t":"opus reticulatum / opus incertum","d":"Net-patterned and irregular rubble walling; the first looks better, the second is stronger."},{"t":"diatonoi","d":"Single stones running through the whole thickness of a wall to bond its faces."},{"t":"isodomum","d":"Greek walling in which all courses are of equal height; pseudisodomum has unequal courses."}]},{"i":145,"j":166,"loc":"Book II, Chapters IX–X","title":"Timber: felling, kinds of wood, fir","summary":"Vitruvius says timber should be felled between early autumn and the coming of Favonius, comparing trees in spring to pregnant women, and recommends cutting to the heart and letting the sap drain before felling. He then describes woods by their mixture of the elements: fir is light and stiff but burns easily, oak lasts underground but warps when wet, alder piles endure beneath water as at Ravenna, and cypress, pine, cedar and juniper resist decay. Larch, which barely burns, was discovered when Caesar's troops failed to burn a tower at the stronghold of Larignum. He closes by explaining that lowland fir from sunny slopes of the Apennines is denser and more durable than highland fir from the shaded side.","ideas":["Trees felled in the wrong season are loose and weak, like pregnant bodies","Each wood's behaviour follows from its proportion of the four elements","Larch resists fire, as the siege of Larignum showed","Sun-dried trees make solid timber; shade-grown trees become porous"],"terms":[{"t":"larch (larix)","d":"A dense, bitter-sapped wood from the Po and Adriatic region that resists decay and fire, named after Larignum."},{"t":"clearstock","d":"Knot-free lower part of the fir trunk, split for joiner's work."}]},{"i":167,"j":182,"loc":"Book III, Introduction–Chapter I","title":"Hidden merit and symmetry of the human body","summary":"Vitruvius recalls Socrates' wish that the human breast had windows, so that real knowledge would be visible and not depend on fame, wealth or flattery; many fine sculptors and painters are forgotten for lack of fortune. He therefore publishes his treatise rather than compete for favour with the uneducated, and summarizes Books I and II. He then grounds temple design in symmetry and proportion, giving the proportions of the human body and showing that a man with limbs extended fits within a circle centred at the navel and within a square. Measures such as finger, palm, foot and cubit come from the body, and he reviews the perfect numbers ten, six and sixteen and their use in Greek and Roman coinage.","ideas":["Merit goes unrecognized when knowledge cannot be seen, so the author writes instead","A temple's parts should relate to the whole as a well-formed body's do","The outstretched human figure fits both a circle and a square","Ten, six and their combination sixteen were held perfect numbers"],"terms":[{"t":"analogia (proportion)","d":"Correspondence among the measures of the parts and the whole, judged by a standard part, from which symmetry arises."},{"t":"teleion (perfect number)","d":"Ten for the ancients and Plato, six for the mathematicians, and sixteen in later Roman reckoning."}]},{"i":183,"j":213,"loc":"Book III, Chapters II–IV","title":"Temple types, column spacing, and foundations","summary":"Vitruvius lists the temple plans: in antis, prostyle, amphiprostyle, peripteral, pseudodipteral, dipteral and hypaethral, with examples. He then classifies spacings as pycnostyle, systyle, diastyle, araeostyle and eustyle, noting the drawbacks of each and setting out the module-based rules of the eustyle, which he credits to Hermogenes, also inventor of the pseudodipteral. Column thickness must grow with the spacing, corner columns are thickened, and shafts diminish toward the top according to height, all to correct optical illusion. For foundations he prescribes solid substructures or charred piles in marshy ground, peripterals twice as long as broad, an odd number of steps, and a stylobate raised at the middle by scamilli impares.","ideas":["Seven plan types range from the simple temple in antis to the open hypaethral","The eustyle balances convenience, beauty and strength","Proportions are adjusted to correct deceptions of the eye","An odd number of steps lets the right foot both begin and end the ascent"],"terms":[{"t":"eustyle","d":"The best spacing, with intercolumniations of two and a quarter column diameters and a wider central bay of three."},{"t":"entasis","d":"The swelling given to the middle of a column shaft, for which a figure is promised."},{"t":"scamilli impares","d":"Devices raising the middle of the stylobate so it does not appear sunken."}]},{"i":214,"j":230,"loc":"Book III, Chapter V","title":"Proportions of the Ionic base, capital and entablature","summary":"Vitruvius gives the proportions of the Attic and Ionic column bases, dividing them into plinth, tori and scotia, and explains how the columns are set, with the outer faces of side and corner columns following their diminution. He sets out the cushion-shaped Ionic capital, its abacus and the drawing of the volutes from an eye, adjusting for taller columns. The architrave's height grows with column height because the eye's strength fails through thicker air, and he gives the fasciae, frieze, dentils, corona, tympanum, simae and acroteria. Upper members lean forward a twelfth to look plumb, columns get twenty-four flutes, and lion's heads on the simae spout rainwater only over the columns; the book ends promising Doric and Corinthian temples next.","ideas":["Every member of the Ionic order is fixed by fractions of the column's thickness","Higher members are enlarged because distance weakens the eye's judgement","Parts above the capitals lean forward so they appear upright","Projections look best when equal to their height"],"terms":[{"t":"volute","d":"The spiral scroll of the Ionic capital, drawn by successively smaller quadrants from a central eye."},{"t":"torus / trochilus","d":"The convex and concave mouldings of a column base; the trochilus is also called scotia."},{"t":"acroteria","d":"Ornaments set at the corners and peak of the pediment."}]},{"i":231,"j":248,"loc":"Book IV, Introduction–Chapter I (4.pr–4.1)","title":"Origins of the three orders and the Corinthian capital","summary":"Vitruvius tells the Emperor that, having treated the Ionic order in Book III, he will now give the rules for the Doric and Corinthian. He explains that the Corinthian differs from the Ionic only in its taller capital and borrows its entablature from the other two orders. He then narrates the origins: Dorus built the first Doric fane at Argos, and Ion's colonists in Asia proportioned the Doric column on a man's foot-to-height ratio and the Ionic on the slenderness of women. The Corinthian capital is traced to Callimachus, who saw acanthus growing around a basket left on a Corinthian maiden's tomb; the chapter ends with the capital's exact proportions.","ideas":["Column proportions are modelled on the human body: Doric on a man, Ionic on a woman, Corinthian on a maiden","The Corinthian is a third order produced from the other two, distinguished only by its capital","The acanthus-and-basket story of Callimachus explains the Corinthian capital","Other capital types are variants, not separate orders"],"terms":[{"t":"abacus","d":"The flat slab forming the top of a capital, whose curved faces and size Vitruvius fixes for the Corinthian."},{"t":"volute","d":"The spiral scroll of a capital, likened in the Ionic to curling ringlets of hair."}]},{"i":249,"j":266,"loc":"Book IV, Chapters II–III (4.2–4.3)","title":"Ornaments from carpentry and Doric temple proportions","summary":"Vitruvius derives the ornaments of the orders from timber construction: triglyphs imitate boards covering the cut ends of tie-beams, mutules the projecting principal rafters, and Ionic dentils the common rafters. He rejects the view that triglyphs represent windows and argues that the ancients admitted nothing in the stone copy that could not occur in the wooden original. He reports that Arcesius, Pytheos and Hermogenes avoided the Doric order because the corner triglyphs spoil the regular spacing of metopes. He nevertheless gives his teachers' rules, based on a module, for diastyle and systyle Doric temples, including the triglyph layout, corona, guttae, and the fluting of the column.","ideas":["Stone ornament imitates the parts of wooden roof framing","Nothing should appear in the copy that could not exist in the original","The corner-triglyph problem made some architects avoid the Doric order","All Doric dimensions follow from one module"],"terms":[{"t":"triglyph and metope","d":"The grooved blocks of the Doric frieze and the square panels between them."},{"t":"embatēs (module)","d":"The unit, a fixed fraction of the temple front, from which every Doric dimension is calculated."},{"t":"mutule","d":"A sloping block under the corona, said to imitate the projecting end of a principal rafter."}]},{"i":267,"j":298,"loc":"Book IV, Chapters IV–IX (4.4–4.9)","title":"Cella, orientation, doorways, Tuscan and circular temples","summary":"Vitruvius gives the proportions of the cella and pronaos and explains that inner columns may be thinner if given more flutes, since fluting makes the eye read a column as larger. Temples should face west so that worshippers at the altar look toward the sunrise, unless the site calls for a view of the city, a river or a road. He sets out the rules for Doric, Ionic and Attic doorways and doors, then the plan and proportions of Tuscan temples with their timber beams. Circular monopteral and peripteral temples, plans like those of Castor and Vejovis, and the pseudoperipteral type follow, and the book closes with altars, which face east and stand lower than the statues.","ideas":["More flutes can make a slender column look as thick as a fuller one","Temples face west so that suppliants face the east","Temple plans vary because rites differ from god to god","Altars are high for celestial gods and low for Vesta and Mother Earth"],"terms":[{"t":"pronaos","d":"The porch in front of the cella, bounded by the antae."},{"t":"pseudoperipteral","d":"A plan in which the cella walls are moved out into the intercolumniations to enlarge the cella."},{"t":"monopteral","d":"A circular temple with a ring of columns but no cella."}]},{"i":299,"j":320,"loc":"Book V, Introduction–Chapter II (5.pr–5.2)","title":"Brevity in writing; forum, basilica and senate house","summary":"Vitruvius explains why he writes in short books: technical terms make architecture obscure, his readers are busy, and the Pythagoreans limited treatises to three \"cubes\" of 216 lines so that they would stay in the memory. He then turns to public buildings, contrasting the square Greek forum with the oblong Italian forum, which must accommodate gladiatorial shows. Upper columns should be a quarter smaller than lower ones, imitating the taper of trees. He gives proportions for basilicas and describes in detail the basilica he built at Fano, then the treasury, prison and senate house, whose walls should be girdled with coronae so that voices do not rise and become lost.","ideas":["Short, clear books suit an obscure subject and busy readers","The Italian forum is shaped by the custom of gladiatorial shows","What is above should be lighter than what is below, as in growing trees","Vitruvius's own basilica at Fano serves as a model"],"terms":[{"t":"basilica","d":"A hall beside the forum where business men gather in bad weather."},{"t":"cube (Pythagorean)","d":"A unit of 216 lines; the Pythagoreans allowed no more than three in a treatise."}]},{"i":321,"j":339,"loc":"Book V, Chapters III–IV (5.3–5.4)","title":"Theatre site, the nature of voice, and harmonics","summary":"Vitruvius advises a healthy site for the theatre, since spectators sit still with open pores, and warns against a southern exposure. He gives rules for foundations, cross-aisles, seating lines and exits so that the voice meets no obstruction. Voice is defined as a flowing breath of air moving in circles, like ripples from a stone dropped in water, but rising vertically as well. He then explains harmonics after Aristoxenus: continuous and intervallic movement of the voice, the enharmonic, chromatic and diatonic genera, the fixed and movable notes, the five tetrachords, and the six concords from the fourth to the double octave.","ideas":["A theatre's site affects both the health and the hearing of the audience","Voice spreads in circular waves, which obstructions break up","The three genera differ in how the tetrachord's intervals are arranged","Only six intervals form concords"],"terms":[{"t":"tetrachord","d":"A group of four notes spanning two tones and a semitone, divided differently in each genus."},{"t":"diesis","d":"A quarter tone, used in the enharmonic genus."},{"t":"symphōniai (concords)","d":"The consonant intervals: fourth, fifth, octave, octave and fourth, octave and fifth, double octave."}]},{"i":340,"j":364,"loc":"Book V, Chapters V–VIII (5.5–5.8)","title":"Sounding vessels and the plans of theatres","summary":"Vitruvius describes bronze vessels tuned to the concords and set in niches among the seats, so that the voice from the stage wakes notes in unison and gains clarity; large theatres get three ranges for the three genera. He answers the objection that Rome's theatres lack them, noting that wooden theatres are resonant anyway and citing Mummius's removal of the vessels from Corinth. He then lays out the Roman theatre from four equilateral triangles in a circle, with stage, seating, scaena doors, revolving periaktoi and tragic, comic and satyric scenery. The Greek theatre, drawn from three squares, has a larger orchestra and a higher, shallower stage, and he closes by classing sites as dissonant, circumsonant, resonant or consonant.","ideas":["Tuned vessels reinforce the actor's voice by harmonic sympathy","Roman plans use four triangles; Greek plans use three squares","Symmetry may yield to utility and to the site, at the architect's judgment","Three kinds of scene: tragic, comic, satyric"],"terms":[{"t":"ēcheia (sounding vessels)","d":"Bronze or clay vessels tuned to musical intervals and placed in the seating to strengthen the voice."},{"t":"periaktoi","d":"Revolving triangular machines with three painted faces, turned to change the scene."},{"t":"logeion","d":"The Greek stage on which tragic and comic actors perform."}]},{"i":365,"j":374,"loc":"Book V, Chapter IX (5.9)","title":"Colonnades and open walks behind the theatre","summary":"Vitruvius requires double colonnades behind the scaena as shelter from showers and space for stage equipment, citing examples in Rome, Athens, Smyrna and Tralles. He gives their dimensions and lighter column proportions, distinguishing the elegance proper to colonnades from the dignity owed to temples. The open space between them should be planted, since air from green things, he argues, clears the eyes and draws noxious humours out of the body, as the sun draws mist from open ground. He explains how to drain the walks with pipes and charcoal, and notes that in sieges they serve as stores of firewood, distributed by tribes.","ideas":["Colonnades shelter audiences and store stage gear","Planted open walks are claimed to benefit eyesight and health","Walks serve as fuel stores in war: health in peace, safety in war"],"terms":[{"t":"humours","d":"Bodily fluids whose excess, Vitruvius holds, open air draws out of the body."}]},{"i":375,"j":393,"loc":"Book V, Chapters X–XII (5.10–5.12)","title":"Baths, palaestra, harbours and shipyards","summary":"Vitruvius sets baths in the warmest quarter, with men's and women's hot rooms sharing one furnace and three cauldrons, and describes hanging floors on brick pillars, iron-supported vaults and a domed Laconicum whose heat is regulated by a bronze disc. He then describes the Greek palaestra, though uncommon in Italy: a peristyle two stadia round with rooms for youths, anointing and bathing, recesses for philosophers, the covered xystus, open walks and a stadium. Finally he explains how to make harbours where nature gives no shelter, using pozzolana concrete in cofferdams or blocks dropped from a platform, or drained double cofferdams. Shipyards should face north and must not be built of wood.","ideas":["Bath heating relies on hypocaust floors over a sloping furnace bed","The palaestra combines exercise rooms with seats for learned conversation","Volcanic powder from near Cumae makes concrete that sets under water","Southern exposure breeds shipworm and rot"],"terms":[{"t":"Laconicum","d":"A circular domed sweating room next to the tepid room."},{"t":"xystos","d":"In Greek, a covered colonnade where athletes exercise in winter."},{"t":"cofferdam","d":"An enclosure of stakes driven into the water, within which a wall is built."}]},{"i":394,"j":415,"loc":"Book VI, Introduction–Chapter I (6.pr–6.1)","title":"Learning over fortune; climate and national character","summary":"Book VI opens with Aristippus, shipwrecked at Rhodes, who saw geometrical figures on the shore and won support through his learning; Theophrastus, Epicurus and the comic poets are cited for the view that education outlasts fortune. Vitruvius thanks his parents for his training, says he never sought money or commissions, and explains that untrained men posing as architects drive owners to build for themselves. He then argues that houses must suit climate, since the sun's course shapes bodies, voices and minds: northerners are tall, full-blooded, brave and slow-witted, southerners small, quick-witted and timid. Italy, lying between, combines both qualities, which he presents as the divine reason for Rome's right to rule.","ideas":["Learning is property that survives shipwreck","A gentleman architect waits to be asked rather than seeking work","Voice pitch varies by latitude, compared to a sambuca's strings","Italy is presented as the temperate mean between north and south"],"terms":[{"t":"sambuca","d":"A triangular stringed instrument used as an image of the sky's inclination from south to north."}]},{"i":416,"j":437,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters II–IV (6.2–6.4)","title":"Symmetry, room proportions and exposures","summary":"Vitruvius holds that an architect must first fix a standard of symmetry and then modify it for the site, since the eye is deceived, as by painted scenery or oars that look bent in water. He describes five types of cavaedium (Tuscan, Corinthian, tetrastyle, displuviate, testudinate) and gives proportions for atriums, alae, tablinum, fauces, peristyles, dining rooms and the Corinthian, Egyptian and Cyzicene oeci. Proportions must scale with size, or small rooms become useless and large ones monstrous. He then assigns exposures: winter dining rooms and baths to the southwest, bedrooms and libraries to the east, summer dining rooms and studios to the north.","ideas":["Fix the standard first, then adjust for site with judgment","Vision can mislead, so additions and diminutions are justified","Room proportions change with the size of the atrium","Each room's exposure follows its use and season"],"terms":[{"t":"cavaedium / atrium","d":"The main hall of the Roman house, roofed in one of five styles around a central opening."},{"t":"compluvium","d":"The roof-opening in the atrium through which rainwater falls."},{"t":"oecus","d":"A large hall or dining room, of Corinthian, Egyptian or Cyzicene type."}]},{"i":438,"j":449,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters V–VI (6.5–6.6)","title":"Houses suited to the owner; the farmhouse","summary":"Vitruvius distinguishes private rooms, entered only by invitation, from common rooms open to anyone, and sizes the house to the owner's station: modest men need no grand atrium, while magistrates need regal courts, libraries and basilicas for public business. Town houses put the atrium first, country seats the peristyle. He then plans the farmhouse: a healthy site, a kitchen on the warm side with oxen facing the fire and the east, a north-lit wine room, a south-lit oil room, a pressing room of set size, raised granaries facing north, and barns apart for fire safety. He ends with a test for adequate light and the need for windows on stairs and passages.","ideas":["Propriety means suiting the house to the owner's social role","Farm rooms are placed by what each product needs: cool wine, warm oil, dry grain","A stretched line shows whether a neighbouring wall blocks the light"],"terms":[{"t":"propriety (decor)","d":"Fitness of a building to its purpose and its owner, referred back to Book I."}]},{"i":450,"j":468,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters VII–VIII (6.7–6.8)","title":"The Greek house; foundations and substructures","summary":"Vitruvius describes the Greek house without an atrium: an entrance passage, a peristyle with the prostas and the women's quarters, and a larger men's peristyle with grand rooms for dinner parties, plus separate guests' apartments. He explains the custom of sending gifts to guests, called xenia, and corrects Roman misuses of Greek terms such as andrones, xystus, prothyra and telamones, explaining the Atlas figure. He then turns to durability: foundations thicker than walls, posts under lintels, relieving arches with strong end piers, and buttresses and saw-toothed counterforts against swelling earth fill. He closes by dividing credit among owner (cost), workman (execution) and architect (proportion).","ideas":["Greek houses separate women's and men's quarters","Roman builders misapply several Greek architectural terms","Arches and buttresses discharge loads and resist earth pressure","The architect alone foresees the finished work before it is built"],"terms":[{"t":"gynaeconitis","d":"The women's part of the Greek house."},{"t":"xenia","d":"Gifts of food sent to foreign guests, and pictures representing them."},{"t":"voussoir","d":"A wedge-shaped stone of an arch with joints radiating to the centre."}]},{"i":469,"j":489,"loc":"Book VII, Introduction (7.pr)","title":"Plagiarists punished and authorities acknowledged","summary":"Vitruvius praises the ancients for recording their thoughts and condemns those who steal others' writings. He tells how Aristophanes, as a judge at Ptolemy's contests in Alexandria, exposed plagiarist poets from memory, and how Zoilus, the critic of Homer, was refused aid and met a punished death. Vitruvius insists he has not stolen titles but gratefully draws on earlier writers, and lists Greek authors on scene painting, temples, symmetry and machines, as well as the few Latin writers. He names the great marble temples at Ephesus, Miletus, Eleusis and the Athenian Olympieum, built partly by the Roman Cossutius, and resolves to write since Roman architects have rarely published; Book VII will treat finishings.","ideas":["Plagiarism deserves punishment; acknowledged borrowing is legitimate","Agatharcus's scene painting led Democritus and Anaxagoras to write on perspective","Roman architects equalled the Greeks but rarely wrote"],"terms":[{"t":"Homeromastix","d":"\"Scourge of Homer\", the surname taken by Zoilus."}]},{"i":490,"j":500,"loc":"7.1–7.2","title":"Laying concrete floors and slaking lime","summary":"Vitruvius opens his account of polished finishings with concrete flooring, the most important of them. He explains how to test and ram the ground, how to avoid walls rising under wooden floors, which oaks to use or avoid, and the successive layers of bedding, broken stone, nucleus and surface of slips, cubes or herring-bone brick, which must be rubbed perfectly level. Open-air floors need double planking, a thicker mix, a slight fall for drainage and yearly oil-dregs against frost, or a layer of jointed tiles sealed with lime and oil. He then turns to stucco: lime must be slaked long before use, and a hoe test shows when it is properly tempered.","ideas":["Floors fail when rigid walls or warping timber move beneath them","A floor is built in measured layers: bedding, rammed rubble, nucleus, surface","Outdoor floors need double planking, drainage fall and frost protection","Unslaked bits of lime blister and spoil stucco; tempered lime sticks to the hoe like glue"],"terms":[{"t":"nucleus","d":"The layer of pounded tile mixed with lime laid over the rammed rubble, on which the finished floor is set."},{"t":"herring-bone pattern","d":"A floor of Tibur burnt brick laid in a zigzag, which must be rubbed flat without gaps or ridges."}]},{"i":501,"j":518,"loc":"7.3–7.4","title":"Vaults, stucco coats, and damp walls","summary":"Vitruvius describes vaulted ceilings built of curved cypress furring strips with flattened Greek reeds or bundled marsh reeds tied on, then rendered and polished. Mouldings should be slender, without gypsum, flat in smoky rooms and in relief in summer rooms. Walls receive a rendering coat, three coats of sand mortar and three of powdered marble; such thick, polished stucco holds fresco colours permanently and shines like a solid silver mirror, while thin stucco cracks and fades. For damp places he prescribes burnt-brick mortar, a ventilated inner wall or pitched hooked tiles over vented channels, and ends with decoration suited to winter dining rooms and the cheap Greek black floor of charcoal and ash.","ideas":["Three coats of sand mortar and three of marble make stucco solid and brilliant","Colours laid on wet stucco bond with the lime and do not fade","Damp walls are isolated by vented cavities or pitched tiles","Decoration must suit the room: plain black panels where smoke and soot gather"],"terms":[{"t":"rendering coat","d":"The first rough coat of mortar applied to a wall or vault before the finer layers of sand mortar and marble."},{"t":"wattle and daub","d":"A wall of sticks smeared with mud, which needs crossed rows of nailed reeds to keep the stucco from cracking."}]},{"i":519,"j":529,"loc":"7.5–7.6","title":"Against fantastic wall painting","summary":"Vitruvius says the ancients painted only what exists or could exist: imitation marble, buildings, stage facades, landscapes, gods, the battles at Troy and the wanderings of Ulysses. He attacks the present taste for monstrosities, such as reeds for columns, candelabra carrying shrines and half-figures sprouting from stalks, which cannot exist and are approved only by bad judges. He tells how the mathematician Licymnius shamed Apaturius of Alabanda into removing a painted scaena with buildings perched on roofs. He blames the decline on lavish colours like vermilion replacing skill, then notes the kinds of marble usable in stucco.","ideas":["A picture should represent a thing that exists or can exist","Fantastic ornament violates propriety however skilful its execution","Licymnius's rebuke of Apaturius's impossible scaena","Costly colours now stand in for artistic labour"],"terms":[{"t":"propriety","d":"Fitness of a work to reality and to its place, the standard by which Vitruvius judges painting."},{"t":"episcaenium","d":"The upper storey of a stage set, on which Apaturius painted buildings and roofs."}]},{"i":530,"j":567,"loc":"7.7–7.14","title":"Natural and artificial colours for painters","summary":"Vitruvius surveys pigments, first those dug from the ground: yellow ochre (best once from the Attic silver mines), red earths, Paraetonium and Melian white, green chalk, orpiment and sandarach. He gives a long account of cinnabar, its yield of quicksilver, the stone that floats on quicksilver while gold sinks, gold recovery, and why vermilion blackens in sunlight unless protected by a coat of Pontic wax. He then describes artificial colours: lampblack from burnt resin, Alexandrian blue from sand, natron and copper, burnt ochre, white lead and verdigris from vinegar, purple from shellfish whose shade varies with latitude, and cheap plant-based substitutes. The book closes by announcing water as the next subject.","ideas":["Heaviness depends on a substance's nature, not its weight, as quicksilver shows","Vermilion fades in open light; a wax coating preserves it","Purple dye's shade varies with the sun's course over its region","Plant dyes and dyed chalk imitate costly pigments"],"terms":[{"t":"ganōsis","d":"The Greek term for coating a painted wall with melted Pontic wax and polishing it to protect the colour."},{"t":"ostrum","d":"Purple dye taken from the shells of marine shellfish."}]},{"i":568,"j":583,"loc":"8.pr–8.1","title":"Water among the elements; finding springs","summary":"Vitruvius opens Book VIII with the philosophers' views of the primary elements, from Thales's water and Heraclitus's fire to the four elements of Pythagoras and Empedocles, and argues that divine intelligence made the necessities of life, above all water, abundant and free. He then explains how to find water: watching for rising vapour at dawn with the chin on the ground, judging the soil (clay, gravel, black earth, tufa, mountain foot), and reading plants that grow naturally rather than in rain-filled hollows. He gives tests with an oiled bowl, unbaked pot, fleece, lamp or fire left in a covered pit, and favours northern mountain slopes, whose shade keeps water sweet.","ideas":["All things depend on the elements; water is the chief requisite of life","Soil type predicts quantity, depth and taste of water","Overnight tests in a covered pit reveal hidden moisture","Shaded northern mountains yield the best springs; flat plains the worst"],"terms":[{"t":"four elements","d":"Air, fire, earth and water, which Pythagoras, Empedocles and others held to be the primordial constituents of things."}]},{"i":584,"j":593,"loc":"8.2","title":"Rain, winds, and the sources of rivers","summary":"Vitruvius argues that rainwater is most wholesome because the sun draws up the lightest, purest part of waters, which winds carry as clouds until they break against mountains. He compares the process to vapour rising and dripping from the vaults of hot baths, and to sweat from a heated body. Damp south winds bring rain to the north, and he lists great rivers that rise in the north, tracing the Nile from the Atlas range through Meroë into Egypt. He concludes that northern springs are better unless tainted by sulphur, alum or asphalt, and that heat is not a property of water but is picked up from hot ground.","ideas":["Clouds form from vapour lifted by solar heat and break on mountains","The bath-vault analogy for evaporation and rainfall","Most great rivers rise in the north, including, he claims, the Nile in Mauretania","Hot springs are cold water heated in passage, so they cool again"],"terms":[]},{"i":594,"j":607,"loc":"8.3.1–8.3.13","title":"Hot, mineral, oily, and petrifying waters","summary":"Vitruvius explains hot springs as sweet water boiled by underground fire in alum, asphalt or sulphur, and cold foul springs as water that has passed burning places and cooled, illustrating bubbling springs with a covered bronze vase on the fire. He lists the healing powers of sulphur, alum, asphalt and natron springs and the harm of waters from mines, which cause cramp and gout. He catalogues salt lakes, oily rivers, pitch and asphalt springs (Semiramis's wall at Babylon), a Cappadocian lake that turns reeds to stone, and the crusting springs of Hierapolis. He traces all such differences to juices in the soil, as with the distinct wines and aromatic plants of each region.","ideas":["Water takes its qualities from the soils and fires it passes through","Hot mineral springs heal; waters from metal mines harm","Petrifying and asphalt-bearing waters have natural causes","Regional wines and spices show the soil's juices at work, governed by the sun"],"terms":[]},{"i":608,"j":625,"loc":"8.3.14–8.4.2","title":"Marvellous springs and tests of good water","summary":"Vitruvius continues with waters that colour sheep's fleeces, deadly springs such as the Water of the Styx said to have killed Alexander, acid waters that dissolve bladder stones as vinegar dissolves eggshell, lead and lava, and intoxicating, goitre-causing and mind-dulling springs, quoting Greek epigrams from Clitor, Zea and Susa. He recalls his guest Caius Julius, Masinissa's son, describing springs that give fine voices. He names his sources, Theophrastus, Timaeus, Posidonius and others, and insists on care in choosing springs for health. Good water is proved by the healthy physique of local people, by leaving no stain on bronze or sediment when boiled, and by cooking vegetables quickly.","ideas":["Water's effects on bodies come from juices it absorbs in the earth","Acid waters' action is inferred from experiments with vinegar","Epigrams at famous springs warn of their effects","Test water by the health of those who drink it and by bronze and boiling"],"terms":[{"t":"Water of the Styx","d":"An extremely cold water at Nonacris in Arcadia that, by tradition, shatters any vessel but a mule's hoof."}]},{"i":626,"j":645,"loc":"8.5–8.6","title":"Levelling, aqueducts, wells, and cisterns","summary":"Vitruvius prefers the chorobates, a twenty-foot straightedge with plumblines and a water groove, to other levelling instruments, answering Archimedes's objection that water's surface is curved. He sets out three ways to conduct water: arched masonry channels with a minimum gradient and a three-part city reservoir for fountains, baths and private houses; lead pipes, sized by plate width and laid across valleys with a level venter; and cheaper clay pipes jointed with lime and oil. He condemns lead as unwholesome, citing the pallor of plumbers. He closes with wells, testing for deadly vapours with a lamp, and cisterns of signinum work with settling compartments.","ideas":["Accurate levelling comes first; the chorobates is most reliable","City water is divided among public fountains, baths and paying houses","Clay pipes are healthier and easier to repair than lead","A lowered lamp detects suffocating air in wells"],"terms":[{"t":"chorobates","d":"A long levelling straightedge on legs, checked by plumblines or water in a groove."},{"t":"venter (koilia)","d":"The level stretch of pipe across the bottom of a valley that lets water rise gently up the far side."},{"t":"signinum work","d":"A hard mortar of sand, lime and broken lava beaten into a trench to line cisterns."}]},{"i":646,"j":668,"loc":"9.pr","title":"Honour due to authors and their discoveries","summary":"Vitruvius complains that Greek athletes win lifelong honours while authors, whose work benefits all nations forever, receive none. He illustrates their usefulness with Plato's doubling of the square by its diagonal, Pythagoras's 3-4-5 right triangle and its use in laying out staircases, Archimedes's detection of silver in Hiero's crown by displaced water, and the solutions of Archytas and Eratosthenes to the Delian problem of doubling the cube. Writers such as Ennius, Lucretius, Cicero and Varro live on through their books, whose opinions outweigh those of living men. He announces that this book treats dialling by the shadows of the gnomon.","ideas":["Minds trained by authors serve humanity more than athletes' bodies","Plato's doubling of the square cannot be done by arithmetic","The 3-4-5 triangle gives a true right angle and stair proportions","Archimedes's 'Eureka' and the test of Hiero's crown"],"terms":[{"t":"mesolabe","d":"The instrument Eratosthenes used to solve the doubling of the cube."}]},{"i":669,"j":687,"loc":"9.1","title":"The zodiac and the courses of planets","summary":"Vitruvius explains that equinoctial shadows differ by place, which is why dials are drawn from an analemma. He describes the heaven turning on its poles about the central earth, the oblique belt of twelve signs, six always above the horizon, and the moon, sun and five planets moving against the heaven from west to east, with their periods. He rejects the view that planets retrograde because they lie in darkness far from the sun, arguing instead that the sun's heat, radiating in a triangle to the fifth sign, attracts and holds them back, citing Euripides's Phaethon. He compares planets to ants on a potter's wheel and explains their heat or cold by position.","ideas":["The heaven revolves about fixed poles with earth at the centre","Planets move opposite to the heaven at different speeds by orbit size","Retrogradation explained by the sun's triangular rays, not darkness","Ants walking against a turning potter's wheel model planetary motion"],"terms":[{"t":"analemma","d":"A geometric figure derived from the sun's course and the gnomon's shadow, used to lay out sundials."},{"t":"poloi (poles)","d":"The two pivots of the heaven's axis, one high in the north and one hidden beneath the earth."}]},{"i":688,"j":715,"loc":"9.2–9.6","title":"Moon, sun's path, and the constellations","summary":"Vitruvius gives two accounts of the moon's phases: Berosus's half-luminous ball turning toward the sun, and Aristarchus's moon as a mirror lit by the sun, tracing the days from new to full to waning. He follows the sun through the twelve signs, marking the equinoxes and solstices at one eighth of Aries, Cancer, Libra and Capricornus and the lengthening and shortening of days. He then maps the northern constellations, from the Bears, Charioteer and Perseus to the Serpent and the star Polus, and the southern ones, from the Centaur and Argo to Orion and the Whale, following Democritus. He leaves astrology to the Chaldeans and weather prediction to the astronomers.","ideas":["Two rival theories of lunar phases, Berosus's and Aristarchus's","Equinoxes and solstices fall in the eighth part of four signs","Constellations described by their positions relative to one another","Unseen southern stars, such as Canopus, are known from travellers"],"terms":[{"t":"bruma","d":"The winter solstice, named from the shortness of the day when the sun is in Capricornus."}]},{"i":716,"j":739,"loc":"9.7–9.8","title":"Drawing the analemma; sundials and water clocks","summary":"Vitruvius gives equinoctial shadow ratios for Rome, Athens, Rhodes, Tarentum and Alexandria, then constructs the analemma step by step with compasses: meridian, horizon, equinoctial, summer and winter rays, axis and circle of months. Declining to invent new dials, he lists inventors of known types, such as Berosus's semicircle and Aristarchus's scaphe. He tells how Ctesibius, a barber's son, discovered pneumatic principles from a counterweighted mirror and built water clocks with float, rack and toothed drums, adjusted for varying hours by wedges or a revolving column. He ends with the anaphoric clock and a stopcock-like regulator whose flow varies through the signs.","ideas":["Each place's equinoctial shadow sets the geometry of its dials","Inventors of dial types are credited, not appropriated","Ctesibius discovered air pressure from a falling weight in a pipe","Water clocks vary flow or scale to produce seasonal hours"],"terms":[{"t":"gnomon","d":"The upright whose shadow marks the sun's position on a dial."},{"t":"anaphoric clock","d":"A water-driven dial whose rotating drum of the zodiac shows hours of varying length."}]},{"i":740,"j":768,"loc":"10.pr–10.2","title":"Machines defined; hoisting engines","summary":"Vitruvius praises the Ephesian law making architects liable for cost overruns beyond a quarter, wishing Rome had it. He defines a machine and distinguishes climbing, pneumatic and hoisting kinds, and machines needing many workers from engines worked by one; all machinery imitates the revolving heavens and underlies weaving, ploughing, presses and transport. He describes hoisting machines: the two-legged trispast and pentaspast with windlass, larger ones with drums or treadwheels, and the single-mast polyspast. He tells how Chersiphron and Metagenes rolled column shafts and architraves from the quarry, how Paconius bankrupted himself, and how the shepherd Pixodorus found the marble quarry.","ideas":["Architects should be bound by their estimates, as at Ephesus","Machines need many hands; engines, one skilled workman","All machinery takes its model from the revolving firmament","Chersiphron rolled shafts on pivots; Paconius's variant failed"],"terms":[{"t":"trispast","d":"A hoisting machine with three sheaves; with five it is a pentaspast."},{"t":"polyspast","d":"A single-timber hoist with many rows of sheaves, quick but needing experts."},{"t":"artemon","d":"The Latin name for the block at the foot of the polyspast through which men haul."}]},{"i":769,"j":798,"loc":"10.3–10.7","title":"Lever principles and water-raising engines","summary":"Vitruvius explains that all lifting combines straight-line and circular motion, illustrating with the lever on its fulcrum, the steelyard, the steering oar, sails set high on the mast, oars, porters' poles and ox yokes. He then describes water-raising engines: the treadwheel tympanum that lifts much water a little way, the wheel with boxes, the chain of buckets, river-driven wheels with floatboards, and the geared water mill. He gives the construction of the water screw, wound from pitched withes on a beam and set at the slope of a 3-4-5 triangle. He ends with Ctesibius's bronze force pump with pistons, valves and a vertical pipe, leaving his amusing devices to Ctesibius's own writings.","ideas":["Rectilinear and circular motion together produce all lifting","Distance from the centre multiplies force, as in lever and steelyard","Wheels, buckets and screws trade height against quantity","Ctesibius's pump forces water upward through valves"],"terms":[{"t":"hypomochlion (fulcrum)","d":"The support about which a lever turns."},{"t":"tympanum","d":"A compartmented wheel turned by treading that lifts large quantities of water a short height."}]},{"i":799,"j":815,"loc":"10.8–10.9","title":"The water organ and the hodometer","summary":"Vitruvius describes the water organ: pumps worked by levers force air through dolphin-hung valves into a regulator standing in water, then into a windchest with channels and cocks; keys move oiled sliders that open holes to the pipes, producing melodies. He admits the subject is obscure and best understood by seeing the instrument. He then explains the hodometer for carriages: a four-foot wheel turns a one-toothed drum that advances a four-hundred-tooth drum, which in turn moves a horizontal drum dropping a pebble into a bronze vessel each mile. The same gearing, driven by paddle wheels, measures distance at sea.","ideas":["Compressed air regulated by water feeds the organ's pipes","Keys work sliders that open and close air holes","Gear reduction of 400 to 1 counts miles by falling pebbles","The device adapts to ships with paddle wheels"],"terms":[{"t":"kanōn mousikos","d":"The main body of the water organ, the windchest with its channels."},{"t":"hodometer","d":"A geared device that counts distance travelled by carriage or ship."}]},{"i":816,"j":838,"loc":"10.10–10.12","title":"Catapults, ballistae, and their tuning","summary":"Vitruvius sets out the proportions of the scorpio or catapult, all reckoned in multiples of the hole in the capital, itself one ninth of the arrow's length, giving dimensions for capital, posts, pipe, windlass, trigger, standard and arms. He notes the trade-off between high- and low-tensioned capitals and arm length, likened to longer and shorter levers. For ballistae the hole is sized by the weight of the stone, and he tabulates holes from two to 360 pounds before giving the parts; several figures are missing in the text. He ends by explaining how the twisted strings are tightened by windlass and tuned by ear until both sides sound alike.","ideas":["Every part of an engine is a fixed multiple of one module","Catapults scale to arrow length, ballistae to stone weight","Taller capitals call for shorter arms, lower ones for longer","Strings are tuned to equal pitch by musical hearing"],"terms":[{"t":"scorpio","d":"An arrow-shooting torsion engine, a kind of catapult."},{"t":"ballista","d":"A torsion engine for throwing stones, sized by the weight of its missile."}]},{"i":839,"j":860,"loc":"10.13–10.15","title":"Siege towers, rams, and tortoises","summary":"Vitruvius tells how the battering ram began at the Carthaginian siege of Cadiz and was developed by the Tyrian carpenter Bright, Geras of Chalcedon, Polyidus, and Alexander's engineers Diades and Charias. He reports Diades's moveable towers of ten and twenty stories, his ram tortoise with water stored against fire, and his borer, noting that Diades dismissed the raven and never explained his scaling machine. He then gives his own teachers' design for a ditch-filling tortoise on steerable wheels covered with stuffed rawhide, variants with parapets or wedge-shaped fronts for miners, and Hegetor of Byzantium's huge tortoise carrying a 180-foot ram movable in six directions.","ideas":["The ram grew from a hand-held beam to wheeled, roofed machines","Diades's towers were tapered, multistoried and hide-covered","Tortoises protect men filling ditches or mining walls","Hegetor's ram could reach walls a hundred feet high"],"terms":[{"t":"tortoise (testudo)","d":"A wheeled, roofed shelter protecting soldiers and rams as they approach a wall."},{"t":"eschara","d":"The Greek term for the base frame of a tortoise or ballista."}]},{"i":861,"j":874,"loc":"10.16","title":"Defence by cunning rather than machines","summary":"Vitruvius declines to describe simple siege gear or defensive works, since besiegers do not follow books and clever plans often defeat machines. He tells how the Rhodians gave Diognetus's stipend to Callias for a crane model, but Callias could not seize Demetrius's giant helepolis, since not everything works at full scale; Diognetus then flooded the ground with water and filth so the helepolis stuck fast, and he dedicated it to the people. He adds the Chians grounding ships on a submerged mound, Trypho at Apollonia locating mines by ringing bronze vessels, and Marseilles flooding mines and snaring a ram. He closes the treatise, claiming ten books cover all branches of architecture.","ideas":["What works in a model may fail when enlarged","Diognetus defeated the helepolis by turning the ground to swamp","Ringing bronze vessels revealed enemy mines at Apollonia","Wise plans, more than machines, preserve cities"],"terms":[{"t":"helepolis","d":"A huge moveable siege tower, like the one Epimachus built for Demetrius against Rhodes."}]}]});